WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.230
Deviations are departures
from approved procedures,

2
00:00:03.230 --> 00:00:06.980
established processes
or expected conditions.

3
00:00:06.980 --> 00:00:09.510
A robust pharmaceutical
quality system encourages

4
00:00:09.510 --> 00:00:13.160
prompt reporting and
proportionate investigation.

5
00:00:13.160 --> 00:00:17.960
The purpose is to understand what
happened, assess potential product impact

6
00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:22.990
and prevent recurrence. Corrective
and preventive action, or C A P

7
00:00:22.990 --> 00:00:26.880
A, is one possible output of
a documented investigation.

8
00:00:27.580 --> 00:00:32.420
When an unexpected event is detected,
personnel should first protect people,

9
00:00:32.420 --> 00:00:36.720
product and evidence as appropriate
to the situation. This may require

10
00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:41.420
stopping an activity, segregating
affected materials or placing a batch on

11
00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:44.590
hold under approved procedures.
Immediate containment

12
00:00:44.590 --> 00:00:47.500
controls the potential consequences while

13
00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:53.460
the quality assessment continues. Personnel
must not silently correct or conceal an event.

14
00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:59.140
Deviation records should capture what
happened, when and where it occurred,

15
00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:03.880
the affected equipment or materials, and
the immediate actions taken. Classification

16
00:01:03.880 --> 00:01:10.420
and investigation depth should reflect
risk, potential impact and site procedures.

17
00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:16.740
Records should distinguish observed facts from
assumptions. Clear contemporaneous documentation supports

18
00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:19.480
reliable evaluation and later review.

19
00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:24.700
Root cause investigation seeks to
explain why a deviation occurred, not

20
00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:29.280
simply identify who was present.
Teams may use a timeline, process

21
00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:34.660
mapping, the five whys or a cause-and-effect
diagram when suitable. Potential

22
00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:39.700
causes should be tested against
available evidence. Human error alone is

23
00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:45.140
rarely an adequate explanation if procedure
design, training, equipment or working

24
00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:46.380
conditions contributed.

25
00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:49.810
Product impact assessment
considers the affected

26
00:01:49.810 --> 00:01:53.560
materials, batches, processes, time window

27
00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:58.800
and available supporting data. The assessment
should be scientifically justified and

28
00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:01.710
documented by authorized personnel. It may

29
00:02:01.710 --> 00:02:04.780
require additional
investigation or testing,

30
00:02:04.780 --> 00:02:09.060
but testing alone cannot always
establish that a process deviation had

31
00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:12.490
no impact. Final disposition
follows applicable

32
00:02:12.490 --> 00:02:15.170
procedures and regulatory requirements.

33
00:02:15.870 --> 00:02:20.350
A correction addresses an identified
problem, while a corrective action aims

34
00:02:20.350 --> 00:02:25.090
to remove its cause and prevent
recurrence. Preventive actions may address

35
00:02:25.090 --> 00:02:30.050
potential problems before they occur.
Actions should have clear ownership, due

36
00:02:30.050 --> 00:02:35.450
dates and documented implementation.
Retraining may be appropriate in some cases,

37
00:02:35.450 --> 00:02:39.660
but should not be the default response
without understanding the underlying cause.

38
00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:44.520
CAPA effectiveness should be evaluated
using measures suited to the identified

39
00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:48.030
cause and risk. Examples include observing

40
00:02:48.030 --> 00:02:50.920
process performance,
reviewing repeat events

41
00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:55.220
or verifying that a new control
works as intended. Trend analysis

42
00:02:55.220 --> 00:02:58.370
can identify recurring deviations that

43
00:02:58.370 --> 00:03:01.580
individual investigations
miss. An action should

44
00:03:01.580 --> 00:03:05.530
not be declared effective solely
because it was completed on schedule.

45
00:03:06.230 --> 00:03:10.550
Imagine a filling-line differential pressure
alarm that occurred during production and

46
00:03:10.550 --> 00:03:15.050
was not promptly documented. The event
should be reported, relevant records

47
00:03:15.050 --> 00:03:20.210
preserved and the affected period assessed.
Authorized personnel should investigate the

48
00:03:20.210 --> 00:03:25.550
alarm, the response and potential
product impact. CAPA, if needed, should

49
00:03:25.550 --> 00:03:30.330
address the demonstrated causes, with
effectiveness checks. A return to normal

50
00:03:30.330 --> 00:03:33.260
pressure does not erase
the original deviation.
